# Regression test: a job that was ever `pause`d and is later `terminate`d
# never got reaped from jobs, even though the external process it wrapped was
# genuinely dead.
#
# Root cause: `ThreadStore::spawn` (threads.rs) registers its own control channel for
# every worker thread it spawns -- including `cmd`'s own dispatch thread -- purely for
# `InterruptibleJoinHandle::join`'s internal bookkeeping. Nothing inside that thread's
# own work (e.g. cmd.rs's wait_for_child_inner, which uses a completely separate,
# explicitly-registered control channel of its own) ever reads from it. But
# pause/terminate broadcast to *every* controller registered for a job,
# which includes this otherwise-unconsumed channel too -- so a single pause
# leaves a stale message sitting in it forever.
#
# `InterruptibleJoinHandle::join` (called by `ThreadStore::reap`, well after the thread
# has actually finished) used to race that stale control message against the thread's
# own, by-then-genuinely-ready real result via a plain `select!` -- which picks fairly/
# arbitrarily among whichever channels are already ready, with no notion of one being
# more "current" than the other. Half the time (roughly), it returned the stale `Pause`
# message instead of the real result, and `join_one` treats `Either::Right(Pause)` as
# "still legitimately running, just paused" and re-registers the thread -- permanently,
# since the real result was already consumed by nobody and is now gone from the
# channel's perspective... no, actually still buffered, but never looked at again since
# the thread is presumed still running. Either way: never reaped.
#
# Fixed by having `join` check the result channel first via a non-blocking `try_recv`
# before ever racing the two -- breaking the tie in the result's favor, matching
# `try_join`'s (already correct) existing behavior.
$job_id := $(cmd sleep 6234 &)
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)
pause $job_id
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)
terminate $job_id
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=500)

# Matched by a distinctive sleep duration rather than by id: ids get recycled (see
# tests/job_orphaning_on_parent_death.crush), so a later job here could plausibly be
# handed $job_id's own freed id.
$remaining_rows := $(jobs | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 6234"} | materialize)
$remaining_count := $($remaining_rows | count)
assert ($remaining_count == 0) "a job that was ever paused should still be reaped from jobs once terminated, not linger forever"
echo "done"
